  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Mukesh Singh Kirar vs The State Of Madhya Pradesh on 18 December, 2025 Author: Gurpal Singh Ahluwalia Bench: G. S. Ahluwalia 1 In The High Court Of Madhya Pradesh At Gwalior Before Hon'Ble Shri Justice G. S. Ahluwalia On The 18th Of December, 2025 Miscellaneous Second Appeal No. 86 of 2025 Mukesh Singh Kirar Versus The State Of Madhya Pradesh Appearance: Shri Anil Kumar Sharma - Advocate for appellant. Shri S.S. Kushwaha - Govt. Advocate for respondent/State. Judgment",
    "facts": "This miscellaneous second appeal under Section 71 of Food Safety and Standards Act, 2006 has been filed against the judgment dated 14/08/2025 passed by Principal District Judge, Gwalior / Appellate Authority under the Food Safety and Standards Act, in R.C.A No. 90/2025, as well as order dated 09/12/2024 passed by the Additional District Magistrate / Adjudicating Officer in Case No. Q/Food Safety/112/2023. 2. The facts necessary for disposal of present appeal, in short, are that firm of the appellant was inspected by the Food Safety Officer on 12/03/2023 at 11:00 a.m. On inspection, it was found that 25 kg of Paneer was kept in a freezer, 60 litres of milk was kept in a container, and 25 kg of butter was kept in a drum. They were stored for sale for human consumption. Since the Food Safety Officer had suspicion about the quality of food articles, therefore, samples were collected and they were sent to the Food Analyst. The Food Analyst, by his report dated 12/06/2023, informed that the 2 MSA-86-2025 Paneer was of substandard quality. A copy of report of the Food Analyst was sent to the appellant with information that he can prefer an appeal, if he so desires, under Section 46(4) of the Food Safety and Standards Act. Appellant made an endorsement that he does not want retesting of the food article from the referral laboratory. Accordingly, the complaint was filed. Being aggrieved by the order passed by Adjudicating Officer, appellant preferred an appeal, which has been dismissed by the Appellate Authority. 5. Challenging the orders passed by Authorities below, it is submitted by counsel for appellant that appellant is a petty trader and penalty of Rs. 1,30,000/- is on a higher side.",
    "arguments": "Penalty should be such that a wrongdoer may not be required to face adverse circumstances.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "Penalty should be such that a wrongdoer may not be required to face adverse circumstances.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": ""
  },
